            "note": "楚风祝融：兽身人面乘两龙（《山海经·海外南经》）；Tripo Studio 图片页纯文字，2 张里选第 1 张",
            "source_image": "StatueChu_1.webp"
          },
          "prompt": "KEY DESIGN: a crouching tiger-like beast with a HUMAN FACE standing on TWO DRAGONS. No human body, no throne.\n\nMANDATORY: ONE SINGLE HORIZONTAL ROW of exactly four separate orthographic views of the SAME object, left to right: 1 FRONT view; 2 LEFT side view (object turned so its front points to the LEFT edge of the image); 3 BACK view; 4 RIGHT side view (object turned so its front points to the RIGHT edge of the image). Same scale and same ground line in all four, wide empty gaps between the views, every view complete and not cropped. Plain flat light neutral grey background (#D6D6D6). No floor, no ground shadow, no text, no labels, no frames, no perspective, no extra objects. No fire, no smoke, no sparks, no lava, no glow halo: hot cracks, ember beds, flame tips and lantern glass are painted only as flat opaque bright orange-yellow colour areas.\n\nSTYLE: stylized hand-painted 3D game environment asset rooted in ancient Chinese culture of the southern Chu kingdom (Warring States, Hubei and Hunan): thick chunky silhouette that still reads as a solid black shape, broad clean faceted planes, two or three dominant colour blocks (dark bronze with green patina, red-and-black Chu lacquer patterns), soft painted gradients, a few medium-size ornaments, sparse wear, soot-darkened edges. No photorealism, no micro-grain, no fine brick seams, no dense cracks, no tiny carvings, no harsh highlights. Anything at ground level is basalt, stone or cast iron; timber only high up on buildings.\n\nOBJECT (24m wide x 24m tall x 26m deep): A colossal cast-bronze idol of Zhurong, the southern fire god of the Shan Hai Jing: 'a beast's body with a human face, riding two dragons', in the style of Warring-States Chu bronzes and lacquerware. BASE: two thick coiled Chu-style bronze dragons lying side by side, S-curved bodies with broad cloud-scroll bands, heads raised at the FRONT-LEFT and FRONT-RIGHT, tails curling up at the BACK, mouths painted flat orange-yellow. ON THEIR BACKS: a crouching four-legged TIGER-LIKE BEAST with big clawed paws, its flanks inlaid with red-and-black lacquer cloud and phoenix-feather patterns; at its front rises a HUMAN HEAD on a thick neck (no human torso, no arms): a calm stern ancient Chinese face with long narrow eyes and a short beard, wearing a very tall Chu plaque crown with a small flame-shaped finial painted flat orange-yellow at its tip. The tail curls up behind like a flame-cloud scroll. ASYMMETRY: the head turns slightly to its right, the right front paw rests raised on the right dragon's head. Big simple forms. PROPORTIONS: FRONT and BACK views about 0.95 times as wide as tall; LEFT and RIGHT views about 1.1 times as wide as tall.",
